    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d503">

                <pb facs="0381" n="377" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg377"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.003/898: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-06-27T14:00:00+09:00">June 27, 1938—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 27—7:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>416. Department’s 211, June 25, 2 p.m.—extraterritorial status of Americans.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Representations as desired by the Department were made to the Foreign
                        Office today.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d503-f1"/> We referred to the Shanghai
                        case described in the Department’s 384, December 30, 1 [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">3</hi>] p.m.<anchor n="86" xml:id="d503-f2"/> and to
                        the interview which I had with Hirota<anchor n="87" xml:id="d503-f3"/> on
                        January 10 recalling that I had stated that the American Government would
                        not recognize or give countenance to any attempt on the part of the Japanese
                        to assert jurisdiction over American citizens. The Foreign Office replied
                        that a telegram had been received over the week-end from the Japanese
                        Ambassador at Washington who reported that the statement of the Japanese
                        Embassy’s spokesman at Shanghai had been prominently carried in the American
                        press and had shocked the American public. The Foreign Office has taken
                        steps to obtain from Shanghai the text <pb facs="0382" n="378"
                            xml:id="pg378"/> of the statement under reference. We intend therefore
                        to make a further approach in the matter either tomorrow or the day
                            after.<anchor n="88" xml:id="d503-f4"/></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>In the absence of any official information on the subject, the Foreign
                        Office was reluctant to express any opinion. We pointed out however that if
                        the Japanese Government were to confirm and approve the statement made as
                        reported, the position of the Japanese Government would be open to most
                        serious implications including that of claiming rights of sovereignty in the
                        areas under Japanese occupation in disregard of treaty commitments and
                        declarations with regard to respecting the territorial integrity of
                        China.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>My British colleague informs me that he is making representations on this
                        matter this afternoon.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai for Hankow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
